Remove variables from the env file, and the template and contract too.

By default only the env file changes: `--env-name`, else `[defaults] env_name`, else `.env`. The run then lists where each name still is, with the flag that clears it. Every byte that is not part of a removed entry stays as it was; the env file is kept as a `.bak` copy first.

Exit codes: 0 removed (or nothing to do with --ignore-missing, or a dry run); 1 only with --check, a name is still somewhere; 2 could not run or refused (a name that is not there, a file it cannot read, no terminal to ask on); 4 you answered no.

Usage: evnx remove [OPTIONS] <NAME>...

Arguments:
  <NAME>...
          Variable names, or quoted globs (`'TEST_*'`). Case-sensitive

Options:
  -n, --dry-run
          Show what would be removed; write nothing. Needs no terminal.

      --env-name <NAME>
          Remove from `.env.<NAME>` instead of `.env`. Defaults to `[defaults] env_name`

      --template
          Also remove from the template (`.env.example`, or `[defaults] example`)

      --contract
          Also remove the `[vars.NAME]` declaration from `.evnx.toml`

      --all
          Remove from every environment file, the template and the contract

      --ignore-missing
          A name found nowhere is not an error (for scripts that run twice)

      --no-backup
          Keep no `.bak` copy of the env files (needs --yes; refused for a file git tracks)

      --allow-dangling
          Remove a name even when a line that stays refers to it (`URL=http://h/${PORT}`). Without it, remove names those lines and changes nothing

      --all-names
          Let a pattern remove every variable in a file (`remove '*'`). Needed with --yes or without a terminal; in a terminal, typing the count does the same

      --check
          Only look: exit 1 if any NAME is still in an environment file, the template or `.evnx.toml`, listing where; 0 if it is gone everywhere.
          
          Read-only, for CI. Always looks everywhere (`.env`, every `.env.<NAME>`, the template, `[vars]`), so it takes no scope flags. Exit 2 means no verdict: a file it cannot read whole.

      --no-color
          Disable colored output

  -q, --quiet
          Print nothing on success. Findings, warnings, errors and requested data still print

  -v, --verbose
          Enable verbose output

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Files:
  -C, --dir <DIR>
          Project folder to work in. Defaults to the current folder

Confirmation:
  -y, --yes
          Do not ask for confirmation

Output:
      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format
          
          [default: pretty]
          [possible values: pretty, json]

      --json
          Print the result as one JSON document (needs --yes or --dry-run; with --check it never asks, so neither is needed).

Examples:
  evnx remove OLD_TOKEN                     from .env only
  evnx remove OLD_TOKEN --all --dry-run     every file, the template and .evnx.toml: preview
  evnx rm 'TEST_*' --env-name staging --yes from .env.staging, without asking
  evnx remove OLD_TOKEN --check             CI: exit 1 if it is still in any env file, the template or .evnx.toml

Full guide: https://docs.evnx.dev/cli/commands/remove
